In order to play an MPEG-DASH audio file with the AAC codec on an ESP32, you will need to use an appropriate library (such as libhelix-aac) and manage buffer states for streaming the audio data correctly.

This is an example C++ code demonstrating how to use the ESP32 and build the firmware to support the AAC decoding from a URL using the libhelix-aac library.

Requirements:

1. ESP32 with Arduino support installed in the Arduino IDE (https://github.com/espressif/arduino-esp32)
2. libhelix-aac library (https://github.com/espressif/esp-adf/tree/master/components/helix/include/helix)
3. ESP32 Audio Arduino library (https://github.com/schreibfaul1/ESP32-audioI2S)
4. An appropriate I2S DAC connected to the ESP32

Please note that the following code is just a template and might require further adaptation to fit in your project setup.

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h>
#include <AudioFileSourceICYStream.h>
#include <HelixAACDecoder.h>
#include <AudioGeneratorAAC.h>
#include <AudioOutputI2S.h>

// Replace with your WiFi SSID and password.
const char *ssid = "your_SSID";
const char *password = "your_WIFI_PASSWORD";

const char *url = "http://path/to/your/aac/stream.mpd"; // Replace with your MPEG-DASH manifest URL.

WiFiClient client;
AudioFileSourceICYStream icy_stream(client);
AudioGeneratorAAC aac;
AudioOutputI2S i2s;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("Connecting to WiFi");
  WiFi.begin(ssid, password);

  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {
    delay(500);
    Serial.print(".");
  }
  Serial.println("Connected!");

  i2s.begin();
  aac.begin(&icy_stream, &i2s);

  Serial.println("Start streaming...");
  icy_stream.open(url);
}

void loop() {
  if (aac.isRunning()) {
    aac.loop();
  } else {
    Serial.printf("Stopping\n");
    aac.stop();
    delay(1000);
    Serial.printf("Starting\n");
    aac.begin(&icy_stream, &i2s);
  }
}
```

