To add texture coordinates to the floor, you would need to specify where on the texture the vertices of the floor should map to. This can be done using the glTexCoord2f() function, which takes two arguments representing the U and V texture coordinates. Here is an example of how you can add texture coordinates to the grass and sand parts of the floor:

```c++
void drawFloor() {
    // enable texturing
    glEnable(GL_TEXTURE_2D);
    // bind the texture
    glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, BoxList2);

    // sand surrounding the grass island
    glBegin(GL_QUADS);
    glTexCoord2f(0.0f, 0.0f);
    glVertex3f(-4.0f, -0.1f, -4.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(1.0f, 0.0f);
    glVertex3f(4.0f, -0.1f, -4.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(1.0f, 1.0f);
    glVertex3f(4.0f, -0.1f, 4.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(0.0f, 1.0f);
    glVertex3f(-4.0f, -0.1f, 4.0f);
    glEnd();

    // grass island
    glBegin(GL_QUADS);
    glTexCoord2f(0.0f, 0.0f);
    glVertex3f(-2.0f, -0.05f, -2.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(1.0f, 0.0f);
    glVertex3f(2.0f, -0.05f, -2.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(1.0f, 1.0f);
    glVertex3f(2.0f, -0.05f, 2.0f);
    glTexCoord2f(0.0f, 1.0f);
    glVertex3f(-2.0f, -0.05f, 2.0f);
    glEnd();

    // disable texturing
    glDisable(GL_TEXTURE_2D);
}
```

Note that the texture coordinates range from 0.0 to 1.0, representing the full width and height of the texture image. The vertex positions are specified in world coordinates, and the glTexCoord2f() calls correspond to the same order of vertices in the glBegin() and glEnd() calls.

