Here is an optimal solution in Java that uses a single loop to count the number of flips required to convert the entire string to all 0's or all 1's and also to count the number of flips required to convert each substring of even length to the same character. The function `minFlips` takes the input string `pnm` as a parameter and returns the minimum number of flips required to make the string a combination of even length substrings such that they all contain the same element:

```
public static int minFlips(String pnm) {
    int flipsToZero = 0; // Number of flips required to convert the string to all 0's
    int flipsToOne = 0; // Number of flips required to convert the string to all 1's
    int prevCount = 0; // Count of the previous character in each substring
    int count0 = 0; // Count of 0's in each substring
    int count1 = 0; // Count of 1's in each substring

    for (int i = 0; i < pnm.length(); i++) {
        char c = pnm.charAt(i);
        int count = (c == '0') ? ++count0 : ++count1;

        // If the current and previous characters are different and the current count is even,
        // we need to flip the previous character to make it the same as the current character
        if (i > 0 && c != pnm.charAt(i - 1) && count % 2 == 0) {
            prevCount = (c == '1') ? count0 : count1;
            flipsToZero += (c == '0') ? count1 - prevCount : count0 - prevCount;
            flipsToOne += (c == '1') ? count1 - prevCount : count0 - prevCount;
        }
    }

    // Return the minimum number of flips required to convert the string to substrings of
    // even length with the same character
    return Math.min(flipsToZero, flipsToOne);
}
```

To use this function, you can simply call `minFlips` with the input string `pmn`:

```
